        "excerpt": "Department of Petroleum Engineering,  Heriot-Watt University  \n35\n88\nField Development Applications of Distributed Pressure Measurement\nIt is now well established that the effective vertical permeability in a reservoir can \nonly be determined by an in-situ test; this is a result of the complicated, macroscopic \nnature of vertical flow especially when shales are present.  Core permeabilities are of \nvery little use in assessing the average vertical permeability based on a rather large \narea, e.g. a typical grid block in a reservoir simulation study.  Vertical permeability \ncan also be identified by interference testing, either vertical pulse testing in one well \nor transient RFT interference testing between two wells, but the reservoir test as \ndescribed by Bishlawi and Moore is the most widely used technique.  It is currently \nstandard practice in history matching of reservoir simulators to obtain effective \nvertical permeabilities in this way.\nThe second paper of major importance in establishing the foundation of RFT usage \nwas the reservoir engineering study of the Thistle field published by Nadir(1).  The \nThistle field is one of the many Jurassic reservoirs in the North Viking"
